["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Fed Credibility Shatters, Options Market Shrugs The Fed&#x27;s credibility broke over a single skipped hike, yet the options market prices meeting days like any other—the cheapest insurance may be an illusion. Excess Returns Excess Returns Sticky Inflation. Cheap Volatility. A Less Predictable Fed. Why Aren’t Markets More Worried? Fed Volatility Options Strategy Inflation Midterm Elections AI Writing forward volatility demand-based inflation zero DTE straddle jawboning financial repression calendarized gamma\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-excessreturns-美联储信誉崩塌-期权市场却当无事发生/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3cac5e72b6049e76-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:02:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Excess Returns</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>Fed Credibility Shatters, Options Market Shrugs</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Fed&#x27;s credibility broke over a single skipped hike, yet the options market prices meeting days like any other—the cheapest insurance may be an illusion.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Fed</span><span class=\"tag\">Volatility</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"China&#x27;s Economic Peak Has Passed: Credit Dries Up, Deflation Looms China&#x27;s share of global GDP peaked in 2021 and has been declining since; credit growth has fallen from 18% to 5%, and real growth may be only 1.5%-2%; the U.S. has outperformed for five consecutive years. The narrative of industrial chain dominance is disproven by data; the future looks more like Japan-style deflation. Geopolitics Decanted with Dmitri Alperovitch Geopolitics Decanted with Dmitri Alperovitch The Miracle Is Over: Inside China&#x27;s Slow-Motion Economic Decay China economy credit PPP demographics U.S.-China competition trade protection PPP / purchasing power parity Impossible Trinity / trilemma Loan Prime Rate (LPR) Special Revenue Bonds Total Factor Productivity (TFP) Demand-side Controls\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-geopoliticsdec-中国经济巅峰已过-美国优势还将扩大/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8de827bd7254104a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">47:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\">Geopolitics Decanted with Dmitri Alperovitch</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>China&#x27;s Economic Peak Has Passed: Credit Dries Up, Deflation Looms</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">China&#x27;s share of global GDP peaked in 2021 and has been declining since; credit growth has fallen from 18% to 5%, and real growth may be only 1.5%-2%; the U.S. has outperformed for five consecutive years. The narrative of industrial chain dominance is disproven by data; the future looks more like Japan-style deflation.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">China economy</span><span class=\"tag\">credit</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Bell Used Einstein&#x27;s Own Tools to Refute His Central Claim The EPR argument forces a choice between an incomplete quantum description and action at a distance; determinism is never an assumption but a conclusion that is inferred. The full logic of Bell&#x27;s theorem must start here. Theories of Everything with Curt Jaimungal Theories of Everything with Curt Jaimungal Tim Maudlin: Quantum Nonlocality Explained FROM SCRATCH quantum mechanics EPR Bell&#x27;s theorem nonlocality history of physics action at a distance EPR action at a distance configuration space reality criterion ontological locality dynamical locality\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-theoriesofever-贝尔用爱因斯坦自己的工具推翻了他的核心论点/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/06123fc0486c117a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">3:18:36</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Theories of Everything with Curt Jaimungal</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>Bell Used Einstein&#x27;s Own Tools to Refute His Central Claim</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The EPR argument forces a choice between an incomplete quantum description and action at a distance; determinism is never an assumption but a conclusion that is inferred. The full logic of Bell&#x27;s theorem must start here.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">quantum mechanics</span><span class=\"tag\">EPR</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"The Quantum Endgame Is Not About Cleaner Qubits, It&#x27;s About Who Can Mass-Produce with Phone Chip Lines The endgame of quantum computing is not about whose qubits are cleaner, but about who can mass-produce using existing semiconductor fabs; silicon spin qubits reuse TSMC lithography, while ion traps are stuck with a hundred thousand laser beams and superconductors with warehouse-sized refrigerators. From First Principles From First Principles Why Spin Qubits Will Win the Quantum Race (Part 2) (EP 55) Quantum Computing Silicon Spin Semiconductors Superconducting Ion Trap AI Auto-tuning transmon valley splitting 2DEG DETR exchange-only qubit\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-fromfirstprinc-硅自旋将赢-百万比特只需一块硅晶圆/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3248f02d6e6be9fe-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"F\"><span class=\"dur\">3:46:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">From First Principles</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>The Quantum Endgame Is Not About Cleaner Qubits, It&#x27;s About Who Can Mass-Produce with Phone Chip Lines</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The endgame of quantum computing is not about whose qubits are cleaner, but about who can mass-produce using existing semiconductor fabs; silicon spin qubits reuse TSMC lithography, while ion traps are stuck with a hundred thousand laser beams and superconductors with warehouse-sized refrigerators.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Quantum Computing</span><span class=\"tag\">Silicon Spin</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Chip Shipments Will Jump an Order of Magnitude: From a Million to 15 Million Broadcom&#x27;s president reveals an AI chip roadmap: from a million units to 15 million, with the &#x27;beast&#x27; being the largest package of 8 stackable compute dies plus 16 HBM modules, and four of the five frontier labs are collaborating. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato Future GPUs Will Look Like This custom chips ASIC advanced packaging HBM Broadcom compute supply chain back-end design services HBM CoWoS scaleup fabric\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-techtechpotato-未来-ai-定制芯片的形态-一只带-16-个-hbm-的巨兽/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7f1cfd222dffd8f1-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">11:10</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>AI Chip Shipments Will Jump an Order of Magnitude: From a Million to 15 Million</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Broadcom&#x27;s president reveals an AI chip roadmap: from a million units to 15 million, with the &#x27;beast&#x27; being the largest package of 8 stackable compute dies plus 16 HBM modules, and four of the five frontier labs are collaborating.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">custom chips</span><span class=\"tag\">ASIC</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"An Explorer Died for a Fictional City, Born from a Political Forgery Percy Fawcett&#x27;s Amazon expeditions are often told as adventure legends, but the real starting point was border disputes fueled by the rubber trade; his narrative of &#x27;respect&#x27; for indigenous people is a gross oversimplification, and the City of Z that obsessed him may rest on a forged manuscript. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class Percy Fawcett, Manuscript 512, and a Lost City in the Amazon, Pt. 1 exploration history Amazon rubber trade indigenous peoples occultism lost city Manuscript 512 Royal Geographical Society rubber boom Mato Grosso City of Z\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-stuffmissed-福西特不是去冒险-是去给橡胶资本画地图/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">34:06</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>An Explorer Died for a Fictional City, Born from a Political Forgery</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Percy Fawcett&#x27;s Amazon expeditions are often told as adventure legends, but the real starting point was border disputes fueled by the rubber trade; his narrative of &#x27;respect&#x27; for indigenous people is a gross oversimplification, and the City of Z that obsessed him may rest on a forged manuscript.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">exploration history</span><span class=\"tag\">Amazon</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Time Is Not an Illusion: Irreversibility Is More Fundamental Than Symmetric Equations Jim Al-Khalili argues that time is real and directed, and that irreversibility is more fundamental than time-symmetric equations; he also proposes using quantum entanglement entropy instead of thermodynamic entropy as the basis for the past hypothesis. Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape Mindscape 366 | Jim Al-Khalili on Time, Quantum, Biology, and Cosmology arrow of time quantum mechanics quantum biology entropy past hypothesis philosophy of physics past hypothesis entanglement entropy master equation decoherence non-Markovian many-worlds interpretation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-mindscape-时间的方向性比时间对称方程更根本/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d69097efcbd03611-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">1:15:16</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>Time Is Not an Illusion: Irreversibility Is More Fundamental Than Symmetric Equations</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Jim Al-Khalili argues that time is real and directed, and that irreversibility is more fundamental than time-symmetric equations; he also proposes using quantum entanglement entropy instead of thermodynamic entropy as the basis for the past hypothesis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">arrow of time</span><span class=\"tag\">quantum mechanics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Most Gazans Have Abandoned Hamas, and the Window for Disarmament Is Opening Ahmed Fouad Alkhatib, a policy scholar born in Gaza, argues with four layers of opposition, two polls, and demographic data that most Gazans have long been anti-Hamas and that Hamas may be forced to disarm in stages; but host Russ&#x27;s pessimism reminds us that disarmament for Hamas equals political suicide. EconTalk EconTalk Hamas and the Future of Gaza (with Ahmed Fouad Alkhatib) Gaza Hamas Israeli-Palestinian conflict poll manipulation demilitarization Middle East geopolitics Shikaki polls NCAG Great Reset UNRWA DSA\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-econtalk-加沙多数人已抛弃哈马斯-缴械窗口正在打开/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f8da698e33560635-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:22:48</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>Most Gazans Have Abandoned Hamas, and the Window for Disarmament Is Opening</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ahmed Fouad Alkhatib, a policy scholar born in Gaza, argues with four layers of opposition, two polls, and demographic data that most Gazans have long been anti-Hamas and that Hamas may be forced to disarm in stages; but host Russ&#x27;s pessimism reminds us that disarmament for Hamas equals political suicide.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Gaza</span><span class=\"tag\">Hamas</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"US Economic Resilience Comes from Consumers Borrowing from the Future Barkin explains why consumption stays strong despite high prices: consumers cut savings, skip bills, and delay loan payments to keep spending; AI investment so far is more about price hikes and crowding out than productivity gains; the Fed may face a long headwind. Odd Lots Odd Lots Richmond Fed’s Tom Barkin on the Surprisingly Resilient Real Economy consumer resilience Federal Reserve AI capex inflation data centers tariffs SEP dot plot R-star forward guidance private label\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-oddlots-美国经济韧性来自消费者向未来借钱/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">31:17</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>US Economic Resilience Comes from Consumers Borrowing from the Future</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Barkin explains why consumption stays strong despite high prices: consumers cut savings, skip bills, and delay loan payments to keep spending; AI investment so far is more about price hikes and crowding out than productivity gains; the Fed may face a long headwind.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">consumer resilience</span><span class=\"tag\">Federal Reserve</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"America&#x27;s Treasury Secretary Threatens Allies with Divine Wrath, Proving He Has No Cards Treasury Secretary Bessent portrays America as an angry god, demanding allies submit as if to Pascal&#x27;s wager. Farrell sees through the editorial: the louder the threat, the clearer it is that America lacks the means to follow through, and allies are already hedging. Hidden Forces Hidden Forces Bessent&#x27;s Wager and Why the World is Calling America&#x27;s Bluff | Henry Farrell trade war dollar system geopolitics US institutions financial sanctions Pascal&#x27;s wager weaponized interdependence flexible realism unitary executive neo-royalism busted flush\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-hiddenforces-美国财长用神罚威胁盟国-反而证明手里没牌/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2b4203c8f6331570-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">51:39</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Hidden Forces</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>America&#x27;s Treasury Secretary Threatens Allies with Divine Wrath, Proving He Has No Cards</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Treasury Secretary Bessent portrays America as an angry god, demanding allies submit as if to Pascal&#x27;s wager. Farrell sees through the editorial: the louder the threat, the clearer it is that America lacks the means to follow through, and allies are already hedging.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">trade war</span><span class=\"tag\">dollar system</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Consciousness Has No Finish Line: The Brain Is a Multiple Draft, Not a Projection Room Dennett rejects the &quot;Cartesian Theater&quot;: there is no single finish line where all sensations converge into consciousness; the brain is a set of parallel judgments that never get pooled into one master copy. Even the question &quot;when did you become conscious?&quot; often has no factual answer. The Partially Examined Life Partially Examined Life Ep. 399: Dan Dennett&#x27;s Model of Mind (Part One) consciousness philosophy mind models multiple drafts cognitive science Cartesian Theater Multiple Drafts Model Phi phenomenon Qualia Homunculus Stalinesque vs. Orwellian\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-pel-笛卡尔剧场不存在-意识不是放映-而是多重草稿/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/5a66360fe1170966-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">47:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Partially Examined Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>Consciousness Has No Finish Line: The Brain Is a Multiple Draft, Not a Projection Room</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Dennett rejects the &quot;Cartesian Theater&quot;: there is no single finish line where all sensations converge into consciousness; the brain is a set of parallel judgments that never get pooled into one master copy. Even the question &quot;when did you become conscious?&quot; often has no factual answer.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">consciousness</span><span class=\"tag\">philosophy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"The Hard Part of Dementia Care Isn&#x27;t the Dementia Patient—It&#x27;s the Caregiver Who Gets Dragged Down The difficulty of dementia care lies not in the patient&#x27;s condition itself, but in the caregivers who are worn down day after day—care turns wives into nurses and forces a post-90s worker to quit. Love is the starting point, but not the support; the system has yet to catch these families. 不合时宜 不合时宜 漫长的告别：失智症、照护者与终将老去的我们 dementia elder care caregivers long-term care insurance aging only children Hogeweyk The Soul of Care informal care long-term care insurance wet-on-wet watercolor\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-cn300980-失智照护的难不在失智老人-而在会被拖垮的照护者/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/bda8c0f9bba83da6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"不\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:00</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">不合时宜</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>The Hard Part of Dementia Care Isn&#x27;t the Dementia Patient—It&#x27;s the Caregiver Who Gets Dragged Down</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The difficulty of dementia care lies not in the patient&#x27;s condition itself, but in the caregivers who are worn down day after day—care turns wives into nurses and forces a post-90s worker to quit. Love is the starting point, but not the support; the system has yet to catch these families.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">dementia</span><span class=\"tag\">elder care</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The Bottleneck in a Fab Isn&#x27;t the Lithography Machine—It&#x27;s the OHT Robots on the Ceiling In a wafer fab, the real capacity constraint isn&#x27;t just the lithography machine—it&#x27;s the OHT vehicles shuttling overhead. The 300mm transition made them indispensable, and today the market is a duopoly of Daifuku and Muratec. Asianometry Asianometry The Little Ceiling Robots Inside a Semiconductor Fab AMHS OHT semiconductor manufacturing wafer fab automated logistics equipment duopoly AMHS / Automated Material Handling System FOUP / Front-Opening Unified Pod OHT / Overhead Hoist Transport Stocker Interbay / Intrabay\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-30-asianometry-别只盯着光刻机-天花板上的-oht-小车也在卡产能/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c6301489e9b3f727-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">20:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Asianometry</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0830</span></div>\n<h2>The Bottleneck in a Fab Isn&#x27;t the Lithography Machine—It&#x27;s the OHT Robots on the Ceiling</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In a wafer fab, the real capacity constraint isn&#x27;t just the lithography machine—it&#x27;s the OHT vehicles shuttling overhead. The 300mm transition made them indispensable, and today the market is a duopoly of Daifuku and Muratec.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AMHS</span><span class=\"tag\">OHT</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"The Treasury Secretary Trades the Bond Market Like a Position — and Prices Will Win US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent is intervening in the bond market, trade, and sanctions with a trader&#x27;s mindset, but inflation and deficits keep prices rising, making his strategy likely hollow. Patrick Boyle Patrick Boyle Scott Bessent Is at War With Prices — and Prices Are Winning! Treasury Fiscal Policy Inflation Federal Reserve Cryptocurrency Trade War TGA (Treasury General Account) On-the-run vs off-the-run bonds Duration neutral Term premium Stablecoin\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-30-patrickboyle-贝森特与价格的战争-财政部长的宏观赌局/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2eb71149af422d0e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">44:51</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Patrick Boyle</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0830</span></div>\n<h2>The Treasury Secretary Trades the Bond Market Like a Position — and Prices Will Win</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent is intervening in the bond market, trade, and sanctions with a trader&#x27;s mindset, but inflation and deficits keep prices rising, making his strategy likely hollow.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Treasury</span><span class=\"tag\">Fiscal Policy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Elite Universities Are a Cartel: Selling Status, Not Education America&#x27;s elite universities are not charitable educational institutions but a cartel that maintains its brand by freezing enrollment, cultivating alumni donations, and securing federal research grants; they sell status, not education. Modern MBA Modern MBA The American College Cartel University Finance Endowments Elite Education Admissions Insider American Innovation endowment advancement provost tenure yield tech tree\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-30-modernmba-精英大学是制造稀缺的卡特尔-而非教育机构/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3676f3a101faa05c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">45:11</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Modern MBA</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0830</span></div>\n<h2>Elite Universities Are a Cartel: Selling Status, Not Education</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">America&#x27;s elite universities are not charitable educational institutions but a cartel that maintains its brand by freezing enrollment, cultivating alumni donations, and securing federal research grants; they sell status, not education.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">University Finance</span><span class=\"tag\">Endowments</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Phage bet-hedging via 10,000-fold reversion, and first humanized EBV antibodies Phages use SSR-driven 10,000-fold reversion for bet-hedging; humanized EBV antibodies block tumors in vivo; vaccines advance on multiple fronts. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1353: Phage bet-hedging and EBV antibodies phage bet-hedging EBV antibody vaccine cognitive laziness SSR / simple sequence repeat bet-hedging contingency locus hybridoma\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-30-twiv-噬菌体用随机突变下注-ebv人类抗体首次成功/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><div class=\"fallback\">T</div><span class=\"dur\">1:52:21</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0830</span></div>\n<h2>Phage bet-hedging via 10,000-fold reversion, and first humanized EBV antibodies</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Phages use SSR-driven 10,000-fold reversion for bet-hedging; humanized EBV antibodies block tumors in vivo; vaccines advance on multiple fronts.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">phage</span><span class=\"tag\">bet-hedging</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Phage bet-hedging via replication slippage, and EBV antibodies Slippage is not a replication error but a phage bet: the population always carries a few individuals whose reading frame is shifted by repeats, generating variants not yet selected. Most are adapted to the current environment; when bacterial defenses escalate, the low-frequency backup takes over. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1353: Phage bet-hedging and EBV antibodies phage bet-hedging EBV monoclonal antibody vaccine design science communication simple sequence repeats (SSR) bet-hedging hybridoma HAT medium humanized mice\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-30-twiv-噬菌体靠复制打滑预留变异-赌细菌防御会升级/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://www.microbe.tv/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/TWiV_v3_white.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:52:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0830</span></div>\n<h2>Phage bet-hedging via replication slippage, and EBV antibodies</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Slippage is not a replication error but a phage bet: the population always carries a few individuals whose reading frame is shifted by repeats, generating variants not yet selected. Most are adapted to the current environment; when bacterial defenses escalate, the low-frequency backup takes over.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">phage</span><span class=\"tag\">bet-hedging</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"When Code Is Free, Users Pay for Judgment and Distribution Replit&#x27;s engineering lead offers a test: once code is free, only trust, data, infrastructure, atoms, and networks survive; a non-programmer used domain knowledge to build a $180K/month business in three days. Peter Yang Peter Yang How Non-Coders Are Vibe Coding $100K+ Businesses with AI | Amol Jain vibe coding AI startups SaaS Replit model neutrality internal tools Vibe coding TAM System of record MCP Token maxing\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-30-peteryang-代码成本归零后-用户为判断力与分发付费/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/e9c1f372be129004-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">36:35</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Peter Yang</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0830</span></div>\n<h2>When Code Is Free, Users Pay for Judgment and Distribution</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Replit&#x27;s engineering lead offers a test: once code is free, only trust, data, infrastructure, atoms, and networks survive; a non-programmer used domain knowledge to build a $180K/month business in three days.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">vibe coding</span><span class=\"tag\">AI startups</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI&#x27;s third era is persistent coworkers, and the product cycle is only three months OpenAI&#x27;s product lead Tara Seshan argues that AI products are moving from chat to agents to persistent coworkers, and that building must be designed for model capabilities two to three months out; the future of work is steering, not rowing, and the part of writing that is thinking will never be automated. Lenny&#x27;s Podcast Lenny&#x27;s Podcast AI’s third era: the rise of persistent AI coworkers | Tara Seshan (OpenAI’s product lead) product cycle persistent coworker Codex writing as thinking product marketing fit persistent co-worker product marketing fit\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-30-lennys-ai-第三纪元是持久协作者-产品周期只有三个月/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/67bb7dafb90c60da-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:21:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Lenny&#x27;s Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0830</span></div>\n<h2>AI&#x27;s third era is persistent coworkers, and the product cycle is only three months</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">OpenAI&#x27;s product lead Tara Seshan argues that AI products are moving from chat to agents to persistent coworkers, and that building must be designed for model capabilities two to three months out; the future of work is steering, not rowing, and the part of writing that is thinking will never be automated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">product cycle</span><span class=\"tag\">persistent coworker</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"AI Earnings Are a Bubble: Costs Deferred, Profits Front-Loaded AI infrastructure profits are realized now, while costs are amortized into the future, constituting an earnings bubble. The stock market will peak before earnings, so investors should be wary rather than chase. Excess Returns Excess Returns The Profits Come Now. The Costs Come Later. Kevin Muir on Whether AI Earnings Are the Bubble AI bubble earnings quality leveraged ETF gold trade policy Earnings bubble Leveraged ETF Off-the-run bond TGA\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-29-excessreturns-ai-盈利是泡沫-成本后置-利润前置/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3cac5e72b6049e76-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:46</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Excess Returns</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0829</span></div>\n<h2>AI Earnings Are a Bubble: Costs Deferred, Profits Front-Loaded</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI infrastructure profits are realized now, while costs are amortized into the future, constituting an earnings bubble. The stock market will peak before earnings, so investors should be wary rather than chase.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI bubble</span><span class=\"tag\">earnings quality</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"1858 New York Poison Milk: Evidence Solid, Yet Officials Ruled It Harmless In 1858, New York&#x27;s adulterated milk was blamed for killing infants; after journalists exposed it, an official hearing ruled it harmless. The affair eventually led to the 1862 state law and the 1906 federal Pure Food and Drug Act. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class SYMHC Classics: Swill Milk food adulteration public health media investigation American history milk safety institutional corruption swill milk artificial feeding temperance movement spent grain\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-29-stuffmissed-1858年纽约毒奶-证据确凿-官方委员会判定无害/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">39:22</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0829</span></div>\n<h2>1858 New York Poison Milk: Evidence Solid, Yet Officials Ruled It Harmless</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In 1858, New York&#x27;s adulterated milk was blamed for killing infants; after journalists exposed it, an official hearing ruled it harmless. The affair eventually led to the 1862 state law and the 1906 federal Pure Food and Drug Act.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">food adulteration</span><span class=\"tag\">public health</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"A Parasite That Gave Tens of Thousands of Americans Diarrhea: Can&#x27;t Wash It Off, Can&#x27;t Culture It, Can&#x27;t Tell If It&#x27;s Alive or Dead The oocysts behind tens of thousands of US diarrhea cases resist bleach and rinsing, and only become infectious after one to two weeks outside the body; labs can&#x27;t culture them or test for viability, so outbreaks are hard to break. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1352: Clinical update with Dr. Daniel Griffin parasite Cyclospora foodborne outbreak public health imported produce Cyclospora cayetanensis oocyst sporulation Guillain-Barré reactive arthritis\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-29-twiv-一种寄生虫让美国上万人腹泻-洗不掉-养不活-验不出死活/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://www.microbe.tv/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/TWiV_v3_white.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">48:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0829</span></div>\n<h2>A Parasite That Gave Tens of Thousands of Americans Diarrhea: Can&#x27;t Wash It Off, Can&#x27;t Culture It, Can&#x27;t Tell If It&#x27;s Alive or Dead</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The oocysts behind tens of thousands of US diarrhea cases resist bleach and rinsing, and only become infectious after one to two weeks outside the body; labs can&#x27;t culture them or test for viability, so outbreaks are hard to break.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">parasite</span><span class=\"tag\">Cyclospora</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Mac Icons Aren&#x27;t About Pixels, They&#x27;re About Metaphor Draw less, and users can put themselves into the icon. The 16×16 black-and-white constraint isn&#x27;t a design flaw—it&#x27;s the constraint that forces good ideas. Y Combinator Y Combinator Susan Kare: Designing Icons &amp; Graphics For the Original Mac Icon Design Design Methodology Early Mac Brand &amp; Logo Steve Jobs iconographer bitmap graphics proportionally spaced fonts cap height Letraset desktop metaphor\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-ycsp-mac-图标不是像素问题-是隐喻问题/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ba08e7524f0aad6b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"Y\"><span class=\"dur\">51:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Y Combinator</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Mac Icons Aren&#x27;t About Pixels, They&#x27;re About Metaphor</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Draw less, and users can put themselves into the icon. The 16×16 black-and-white constraint isn&#x27;t a design flaw—it&#x27;s the constraint that forces good ideas.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Icon Design</span><span class=\"tag\">Design Methodology</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Time Doesn&#x27;t Exist, and the Universe Didn&#x27;t Come From Nothing Cosmologist Brian Keating makes a counterintuitive case: time may not exist, and the universe didn&#x27;t emerge from nothing. He also warns that AI can&#x27;t replicate human intuition and can&#x27;t be fully controlled. Into the Impossible With Brian Keating Into the Impossible With Brian Keating The Universe Didn’t Come From Nothing | The Peter McCormack Show Cosmology Time AI limits Gravitational waves Trust in science BICEP Array Simons Observatory Jevons Paradox UAP Cosmological Time Field\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-intotheimpossi-时间不存在-宇宙并非从无中诞生/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4a6e7a9d85c7b97f-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">1:46:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Into the Impossible With Brian Keating</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Time Doesn&#x27;t Exist, and the Universe Didn&#x27;t Come From Nothing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cosmologist Brian Keating makes a counterintuitive case: time may not exist, and the universe didn&#x27;t emerge from nothing. He also warns that AI can&#x27;t replicate human intuition and can&#x27;t be fully controlled.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cosmology</span><span class=\"tag\">Time</span>\n</div></div></a>"]